-- 048: Deep content + matching exercises for X12 · Final Project & Portfolio. Upscale.
UPDATE public.sessions SET
 content_en = E'> 🚀 **Upscale** session — available to Extended-track learners.\n\n## From learner to hireable analyst\n\nEverything so far comes together here. The final project proves you can run an analysis **end to end**, and the portfolio is how a recruiter sees it in 60 seconds. This lesson is about doing both well.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The final project — scope\n\nA complete project walks the full path on one real dataset:\n\n`Business question → data collection & cleaning → EDA → visualization → insight → recommendation`\n\nPick a question that a decision-maker would actually care about. Scope it so you can go **deep on one question**, not shallow on ten.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The grading rubric\n\nYou are assessed on the whole chain, not just the chart:\n\n| Criterion | What it measures |\n|-----------|------------------|\n| **Problem framing** | Is the question specific, measurable, and worth answering? |\n| **Data handling** | Cleaning, joining, correct transformations |\n| **Analysis depth** | Right methods; goes beyond surface counts |\n| **Insight quality** | Observation → insight → recommendation; the "so what" |\n| **Communication** | Clear visuals and a structured story |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Timeline & milestones\n\n```\nWeek 1 → pick the question + gather data\n↓\nWeek 2 → clean, EDA, and first findings\n↓\nWeek 3 → visualize, write the story, and record a walkthrough\n```\n\nShip a rough end-to-end version early, then improve — a complete draft beats a perfect half.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Building the portfolio\n\n- **Show your thinking, not just the result.** A README that explains the question, approach, and decisions beats a screenshot of a dashboard.\n- **Depth over breadth.** 2–3 strong, complete projects beat 10 half-finished notebooks.\n- **Make it reproducible.** Data source, code, and a clear "how to read this".\n- **Lead with the insight.** The first line of each project README is the finding, not the tech stack.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Presenting for recruiters\n\nPut projects where recruiters look: a tidy **GitHub**, a **LinkedIn** post per project (finding first, one chart, a link), and a one-page summary. Talk about the **business impact**, not just the tools.\n\n> **Mini case study.** Two candidates knew the same tools. One listed "SQL, Python, Power BI". The other posted a project: "How I found the 8% of customers driving 24% of revenue — and the win-back plan." The second got the interview. The work told the story.\n\n---\n\n## Common pitfalls\n\n- **Tool-listing instead of impact.** Recruiters hire outcomes, not syntax.\n- **Only the final artifact.** Without your reasoning, it looks copied.\n- **Ten shallow projects.** Breadth without depth signals nothing finished.\n- **No recommendation.** An analysis that stops at charts is incomplete.\n\n---\n\n## Glossary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|---------|\n| End-to-end project | Question → data → analysis → recommendation |\n| Rubric | The criteria your work is graded on |\n| README | The document explaining a project |\n| Portfolio | A curated set of projects showing your ability |\n| Reproducible | Others can follow your data and steps |\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n1. The final project runs a real question **end to end** — depth on one beats shallow on many.\n2. You are graded on the whole chain: **framing, data, analysis, insight, communication**.\n3. Ship a complete rough draft early, then refine on a **weekly timeline**.\n4. A portfolio should **show your thinking** and lead with the **insight**, not the tools.\n5. Present impact to recruiters — the work should tell the story.\n\n> 💡 Practice with the matching exercises below, then complete your end-to-end final project and portfolio.',
 content_id = E'> 🚀 Sesi **Upscale** — tersedia untuk peserta jalur Extended.\n\n## Dari peserta ke analyst yang layak direkrut\n\nSemua yang selama ini dipelajari bertemu di sini. Final project membuktikan Anda bisa menjalankan analisis **end to end**, dan portofolio adalah cara recruiter melihatnya dalam 60 detik. Pelajaran ini soal melakukan keduanya dengan baik.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Final project — ruang lingkup\n\nProyek lengkap menempuh seluruh jalur pada satu dataset nyata:\n\n`Pertanyaan bisnis → pengumpulan & pembersihan data → EDA → visualisasi → insight → rekomendasi`\n\nPilih pertanyaan yang benar-benar dipedulikan pengambil keputusan. Batasi agar Anda bisa **mendalami satu pertanyaan**, bukan dangkal di sepuluh.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Rubrik penilaian\n\nAnda dinilai atas seluruh rantai, bukan hanya chart:\n\n| Kriteria | Yang diukur |\n|----------|-------------|\n| **Problem framing** | Apakah pertanyaan spesifik, terukur, layak dijawab? |\n| **Penanganan data** | Cleaning, join, transformasi yang benar |\n| **Kedalaman analisis** | Metode tepat; melampaui hitungan permukaan |\n| **Kualitas insight** | Observasi → insight → rekomendasi; "so what"-nya |\n| **Komunikasi** | Visual jelas dan cerita terstruktur |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Timeline & milestone\n\n```\nPekan 1 → pilih pertanyaan + kumpulkan data\n↓\nPekan 2 → clean, EDA, dan temuan awal\n↓\nPekan 3 → visualisasi, tulis cerita, dan rekam walkthrough\n```\n\nRilis versi end-to-end kasar lebih awal, lalu perbaiki — draf lengkap mengalahkan setengah yang sempurna.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Menyusun portofolio\n\n- **Tunjukkan proses berpikir, bukan hanya hasil.** README yang menjelaskan pertanyaan, pendekatan, dan keputusan mengalahkan screenshot dashboard.\n- **Kedalaman di atas keluasan.** 2–3 proyek kuat dan lengkap mengalahkan 10 notebook setengah jadi.\n- **Buat reproducible.** Sumber data, kode, dan "cara membaca ini" yang jelas.\n- **Mulai dari insight.** Baris pertama tiap README proyek adalah temuan, bukan tech stack.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Mempresentasikan untuk recruiter\n\nTaruh proyek di tempat recruiter mencari: **GitHub** yang rapi, satu post **LinkedIn** per proyek (temuan dulu, satu chart, sebuah tautan), dan ringkasan satu halaman. Bicarakan **dampak bisnis**, bukan hanya tool.\n\n> **Studi kasus mini.** Dua kandidat menguasai tool yang sama. Satu menuliskan "SQL, Python, Power BI". Yang lain memposting proyek: "Bagaimana saya menemukan 8% pelanggan yang mendorong 24% revenue — dan rencana win-back-nya." Yang kedua mendapat interview. Karyanya menceritakan kisahnya.\n\n---\n\n## Kesalahan umum\n\n- **Mendaftar tool alih-alih dampak.** Recruiter merekrut hasil, bukan sintaks.\n- **Hanya artefak akhir.** Tanpa penalaran Anda, terlihat seperti menyalin.\n- **Sepuluh proyek dangkal.** Keluasan tanpa kedalaman tak menandakan apa pun selesai.\n- **Tanpa rekomendasi.** Analisis yang berhenti di chart itu belum lengkap.\n\n---\n\n## Glosarium\n\n| Istilah | Arti |\n|---------|------|\n| Proyek end-to-end | Pertanyaan → data → analisis → rekomendasi |\n| Rubrik | Kriteria penilaian karya Anda |\n| README | Dokumen yang menjelaskan proyek |\n| Portofolio | Kumpulan proyek terkurasi yang menunjukkan kemampuan |\n| Reproducible | Orang lain bisa mengikuti data dan langkah Anda |\n\n---\n\n## Kesimpulan Utama\n\n1. Final project menjalankan pertanyaan nyata **end to end** — mendalam di satu mengalahkan dangkal di banyak.\n2. Anda dinilai atas seluruh rantai: **framing, data, analisis, insight, komunikasi**.\n3. Rilis draf lengkap kasar lebih awal, lalu perbaiki dengan **timeline mingguan**.\n4. Portofolio harus **menunjukkan proses berpikir** dan mulai dari **insight**, bukan tool.\n5. Presentasikan dampak ke recruiter — karya harus menceritakan kisahnya.\n\n> 💡 Latih dengan exercise matching di bawah, lalu selesaikan final project end-to-end dan portofolio Anda.'
WHERE session_number = 'X12';

INSERT INTO public.exercises (session_id, type, title_en, title_id, description_en, description_id, starter_code, solution_code, test_cases, hints_en, hints_id, difficulty, order_num)
SELECT s.id, 'matching', 'Portfolio Elements', 'Elemen Portofolio',
  'Match each portfolio element to its purpose.', 'Cocokkan setiap elemen portofolio dengan tujuannya.',
  '{"pairs":[{"left":"README","right":"Explain the question and approach"},{"left":"Problem statement","right":"Frame what you set out to answer"},{"left":"Visualization","right":"Communicate the key insight"},{"left":"Recommendation","right":"Propose a business action"}]}','',
  '[{"id":"tc1","validation_type":"matching","expected_value":"Explain the question and approach","description_en":"README","description_id":"README","points":25},{"id":"tc2","validation_type":"matching","expected_value":"Frame what you set out to answer","description_en":"Problem","description_id":"Problem","points":25},{"id":"tc3","validation_type":"matching","expected_value":"Communicate the key insight","description_en":"Viz","description_id":"Viz","points":25},{"id":"tc4","validation_type":"matching","expected_value":"Propose a business action","description_en":"Recommendation","description_id":"Rekomendasi","points":25}]',
  '["A README explains the thinking","A recommendation drives action"]','["README menjelaskan proses berpikir","Rekomendasi mendorong aksi"]','easy',1
FROM public.sessions s WHERE s.session_number='X12' AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.exercises e WHERE e.session_id=s.id AND e.title_en='Portfolio Elements');

